<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felix Mendelssohn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Felix Mendelssohn Bartholdy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felix Mendelssohn (1809–1847) was a German composer, pianist and conductor born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, the grandson of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> Mendelssohn. His works include the overture to A Midsummer Night's Dream, the Italian and Scottish symphonies, the oratorio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> and the Violin Concerto in E minor. His 1829 performance of the St Matthew Passion launched the revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>'s music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn.jpg" alt="Felix Mendelssohn" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduard Magnus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felix_Mendelssohn_Bartholdy_by_Eduard_Magnus_(1833).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
